used. Unshown T5:3, so still available. However, forming another Chow with T4 might be okay but not necessarily efficient.  \n\nBetter to focus on one or two suits. We have a T suit meld, so maybe continue with T suit? Hand has T4 only. Alternatively, focus on W suit: W4x2, W5, W8. W8 is isolated. W4 and W5 can connect. B suit: B1, B2x2, B4, B8. B2 pair is good for potential pung or sequence with B1/B3/B4. B1 and B2 can form sequence with B3 (2 left). B4 is isolated but can form sequence with B3/B5 or B5/B6, but B5 only 1 left.  \n\nDiscarding decisions: We want to discard tiles that are less useful and keep tiles that can form melds quickly.  \n\nPromising discards:  \n1. Play J2: It's an honor singleton, hard to form a pair/meld unless we draw another J2 (4 left), but J2 pung would give Dragon Pung (2 points) and possibly Seat Wind? Seat Wind is West Wind (F3), not J2. J2 is Green Dragon, not wind. Discarding J2 moves towards All Simples or reduces honors.  \n2. Play B8: B8 is a terminal? No, B8 is not terminal (terminal are 1 and 9). B8 is simple, but isolated, no nearby tiles in hand (B4 is 4 steps away). B8 can form sequences with B6/B7 or B7/B9, but B9 only 1 left, B7 has 4 left. Still, it's a loose tile.  \n3. Play W8: Similar to B8, isolated in W suit, can form sequences with W6/W7 or W7/W9, but W7 and W9 have 4 left each.  \n4. Play B1: B1 is terminal, limits patterns (except Outside Hand, etc.), but can form sequence with B2/B3 (B3 only 2 left). Keeping B1 might allow sequence with B2 and B3.  \n5. Play T4: T4 can form sequence with T3/T5, but we already have a T meld, maybe we want to diversify?  \n\nConsidering efficiency, keeping pairs is good for potential pungs. W4 pair and B2 pair are valuable. Singles that can easily form sequences: W5 (needs W4/W6, we have W4 pair, so W5 with one W4 could make sequence if we get W3 or W6). B1 with B2 pair could make sequence if we get B3. B4 is isolated but could pair with B4 (we have one B4) if we draw another B4 