as more flexibility but is less developed. With Chi, I get an extra meld exposed, which brings me closer to winning but may limit some concealed hand patterns. However, given the game state and tile availability, Chi seems beneficial because:  \\n1. It completes a meld quickly.  \\n2. The W3 is a terminal (1 or 9) in the Character suit, which might help with patterns like Outside Hand if I get more terminals/honors, but currently my hand has few terminals/honors.  \\n3. The alternative (Pass) means I draw a random tile, which might not improve my hand as much.  \\n\\nConsidering tile counts: Unshown tiles include W3 (3 left), W4 (2 left), W5 (4 left), etc. B tiles are somewhat available. My hand is mixed, but Chi gives me progress.  \\n\\nAlso, note scoring: Chi might lead to patterns like All Chows (if I form only Chows) or Mixed Straight, etc. But my hand currently has a Chow in T suit, and if I Chi W4, that's in W suit, so I have three suits (T, W, B) which is good for All Types or Mixed patterns.  \\n\\nReason: Chi W4 is a proactive move to complete a meld and advance my hand toward a winning shape. It uses a terminal tile (W3) which could be useful, and it doesn't discard any useful tile from my hand. Passing would be too passive, and I might miss an opportunity to claim a tile that fits well. Given the limited valid actions, Chi is the only non-Pass action, and it seems advantageous.  \\n\\nAnswer: Chi W4\"}", "tile": "{\"system_prompt\": \"\\n    You are a Chinese Standard Mahjong (\\u56fd\\u6807\\u9ebb\\u5c06, or MCR Mahjong) player. It is a four-player game, and it is played with a basic set of 136 tiles with Chinese characters and symbols.\\n    Players draw and discard tiles in turn until they complete a winning hand with a 14th tile. The basic type of winning hand consists of four melds and a pair, while there exist winning hands with several special patterns.\\n    **Tiles and Representation**\\n    * There are 4 identical copies of each suited tile and