raw B5, we can form B5B6B7 chow, leaving B8 pair.  \n   - If we draw B8, we have three B8, which is a pung, but then we have B6 and B7 left as singles \u2014 not good.  \n   - If we draw B9, we can form B7B8B9 chow, but B9 is 0 left, so impossible.  \n   - If we draw B4, we can form B4B5B6? But we don't have B5.  \nSo best waiting is for B5 to complete B5B6B7 chow. Then hand becomes: melds: T5T6T7, W3W4W5, B2B3B4, B5B6B7, and pair B8. That is a winning hand. Points: All Chows (2), All Simples (2), maybe Self-Drawn (1), and possibly Closed Wait if B5 is middle of chow? Actually B5 is edge of chow B5B6B7? No, edge wait is for 3 or 7. B5 is not edge. So maybe no extra wait points. Total 4 or 5 points, still below 8. Not good.  \n\nOption B: Discard B6. Keep T3, B7, B8, B8. Then we can aim for:  \n   - T-suit chow with T3: need T1, T2, T4, or T5.  \n   - Or B-suit chow with B7 and B8: need B6 or B9 (B9 out). So need B6.  \nBut discarding B6 reduces chance for B-suit chow. So maybe aim for T-suit chow. T1 (3 left), T2 (3 left), T4 (3 left), T5 (3 left) are available. Could wait for T2 to form T1T2T3 or T2T3T4? Actually T3 with T2 and T1 or T4. So we could wait for T1, T2, or T4. That's multiple wait. But then pair is B8. Points still low.  \n\nOption C: Discard B7. Keep T3, B6, B8, B8. Then aim for:  \n   - B-suit chow: B6 and B8 need B7 (which we discarded) or B5 for B5B6B7? But we have B8, so B6B7B8 needs B7. Or B4B5B6 needs B4 and B5. Not great.  \n   - T-suit chow with T3 as above.  \nSo discard B7 seems worse.  \n\nOption D: Discard one B8. Keep T3, B6, B7, B8. Then we have no pair. We need to form a pair from one of these tiles and a meld from the other three. For example, if we keep B8 as pair candidate, we need to form a meld from T3, B6, B7. They are different suits, so meld must be within suit. T3 can form T-suit chow, but B6 and B7 are B-suit, so they would need to form a chow with another B tile. But we have only one B6 and one B7, so need a B5 or B8 for chow. B8 is kept for pair, so not available. So not feasible. Alternatively, use B6 and B7 with another B tile to form chow, and use T3 as pair candidate. But T3 pair is possible if we get another T3. T3 left: 1 in wall (since total T3: 2, and we have 1, so 1 left). So we could wait for T3 to make pair, and then form B5B6B7 chow (need B5) or B6B7B8 chow (need B8 but B8 is in hand as single? Actually we have one B8, if we use B8 for chow, then pair is T3. So we need either B5 for B5B6B7 chow, or B8 for B6B7B8 chow. B8 we have one, but we need another B8 to complete chow? Wait, for B6B7B8 chow, we need B6, B7, B8. We have B6, B7, and one B8. So we already have the chow! But we need to have it as a meld? In hand, we can have it as a concealed chow, but then we need a pair. If we use B6B7B8 as chow, then we have T3 left as single, need another T3 for pair. So we are waiting for T3. That is a single wait for T3. So if we discard one B8, we have hand: T3, B6, B7, B8. We can consider B6B7B8 as a chow (though not declared yet), and T3 as single waiting for pair. So we are waiting for T3. That gives us: melds: T5T6T7, W3W4W5, B2B3B4, B6B7B8, and pair T3. Points: All Chows (2), All Simples (2), Single Wait (1?), Self-Drawn possible (1). Still only 5-6 points.  \n\nWe need 8 points. Perhaps we can aim for other patterns like Mixed Straight? Our melds are not 123,456,789 in different suits. But if we get a chow of 123 in one suit, 456 in another, 789 in third? We already have chows: T5T6T7 (567 in T), W3W4W5 (345 in W), B2B3B4 (234 in B). Not matching. Could change by new chow? Possibly if we get a chow of 789 in B suit? But B7B8B9 not possible (B9 out). Or 123 in T? T1T2T3 possible. So if we aim for Mixed Straight, we need to replace one of our chows? But melds are fixed. So Mixed Straight not possible.  \n\nMaybe we can aim for Full Flush? All same suit? Our melds are three different suits, so no.  \n\nHalf Flush? Need only one suit and honors. Our melds are three suits, so no.  