,B8,B8, wait for T-suit chow tiles or B7 for B-suit chow.  \n\nDiscarding one B8 leaves T3,B6,B7,B8, wait for T3 for pair, or B5 for B5B6B7 chow, or B8 for B6B7B8 chow? Actually if we have B6,B7,B8, that's already a chow, so we wait for T3 to pair. So that is a single wait for T3.  \n\nWhich wait has the most tiles available?  \n- For B5: 1 left.  \n- For T3: 1 left.  \n- For T1: 3 left (but need T1 for T1T2T3 chow, also need T2). Actually for T-suit chow with T3, we need specific tiles: if we keep T3 and discard B8, we wait for T3 (1 tile). If we keep T3 and discard B6, we need T1 or T2 or T4 to form chow, and we also need a pair. If we aim for chow with T3, we might need multiple tiles, so it's not a clear wait.  \n\nGiven the tile counts, B5 has only 1 left, T3 has 1 left, so both are scarce. But B5 might be safer because it's a simple tile and less likely to be discarded? Looking at history, B5 has been played: previous player played B5 twice, next player hasn't played B5, across player hasn't played B5. So there might be B5 in wall.  \n\nBut we need to consider that to win, we need 8 points. Perhaps we should aim for a hand that can include a dragon pung or wind pung? But we have no winds or dragons in hand.  \n\nMaybe we can try to claim a discard to form a pung of J1 or something? But that would require having two J1 in hand, which we don't.  \n\nGiven the situation, it seems unlikely to reach 8 points with current hand unless we get a pattern like Last Tile or Robbing Kong, which are situational.  \n\nBut perhaps we are overthinking: the question is to choose a discard now. We need to choose one of Play B8, Play B7, Play T3, Play B6.  \n\nI think discarding T3 is reasonable because it isolates the T suit and focuses on B suit, where we have more tiles (B6,B7,B8). Also, keeping B8 pair is good because pair is needed. Then we wait for B5 to complete B5B6B7 chow. That gives a clear waiting pattern.  \n\nBut points are low. Maybe we should discard B8 to try to go for a hand with pair T3 and chow B6B7B8, which is also clear wait for T3.  \n\nComparing:  \nWait for B5: 1 tile left.  \nWait for T3: 1 tile left.  \nBoth similar. But which gives more points? If we wait for B5, we have chow B5B6B7, which is not edge or closed wait? B5 is edge of chow? Actually B5B6B7: B5 is the lowest number in the chow, but edge wait is defined for winning tile 3 to complete 123 or 7 to complete 789. So B5 does not qualify for Edge Wait. So no extra point. If we wait for T3 to complete pair, that is Single Wait (1 point). So that gives an extra point. So waiting for T3 gives Single Wait (1), while waiting for B5 does not necessarily give a wait pattern (unless it's closed wait? Closed wait is for middle tile of chow, but B5 is not middle of B5B6B7? Actually B5 is first, so not middle. So no). So waiting for T3 yields potentially one more point.  \n\nThus, discarding one B8 to wait for T3 might yield: All Chows (2) + All Simples (2) + Single Wait (1) + Self-Drawn (1) = 6 points. Still short, but closer.  \n\nMaybe we can also get Concealed Hand if we draw winning tile? Concealed Hand requires all melds concealed and winning tile from other player. Our melds are exposed, so not Concealed Hand.  \n\nGiven that we need 8 points, maybe we should consider discarding B8 to keep T3 and aim for a pattern like Mixed Shifted Chows? Not likely.  \n\nActually, looking at the melds of the player across: they have F2 pung and two chows in W suit. So winds are being used.  \n\nPerhaps we should just choose a discard that keeps flexibility and hope for a high-point pattern later, but it's uncertain.  \n\nI think the most straightforward is to discard T3, keep B8 pair, and wait for B5. That gives a clean hand structure.  \n\nBut let's see the valid actions: Play B8; Play B7; Play T3; Play B6.  \n\nI'll choose Play T3 to focus on B suit.  \n\nEvaluation: Discarding T3 simplifies the hand to focus on the B suit, where we have a pair of B8 and potential chow B5B6B7